on" – it's marketing hyperbole. - Even if a material itself were truly impervious, seams and seals are not. So there's always a point of failure over time/pressure. - Legally, companies protect themselves with those ratings; they don't claim indefinite immersion because they'd lose lawsuits. - So yes, if they slap "waterproof" on the box, they *should* mean no water gets in, but the reality is it's just a grade of resistance with fine print. I'll structure the answer clearly, reinforcing that the word "waterproof" is misleading but technically they only guarantee it within the rated limits. I'll also mention that the FTC in the US has rules about using "waterproof" in marketing, and for watches it's banned unless it's truly impermeable, but for phones they often avoid those strict rules by hyphenating or using IP ratings. I'll keep it friendly and informative.
